Smallpox was declared eradicated in 1980 — the only human disease ever eliminated — entirely through vaccination.
Polio cases worldwide have fallen by more than 99.9% since the Global Polio Eradication Initiative launched in 1988, from 350,000 annual cases to under 30.
Measles vaccination prevents an estimated 21 million deaths per decade; outbreaks occur almost exclusively in unvaccinated populations.
COVID-19 vaccines reduced hospitalisation and death by 70–95% in the first year of rollout, according to peer-reviewed studies in the NEJM and The Lancet.
